By T.S Metcalfe

- Orbital period > 18,000 years so not able to use traditional binary star dynamics - asteroseismology useful
- Solar like - give glimpse into the sun's evolution
- Amongst the brightest stars available from Kepler so able to get good oscillation measurements
- Aims - to detect and characterise solar-like oscillations, measure individual frequencies and then model them, test independently and see if they match, assess reliability of asteroseismic modelling for solar-like stars
- Methods - used photometric data from Kepler to get oscillation spectra from light curves, extract frequencies and model using Asteroseismic Modeling Portal, perform independent testing
- Results - produced clean frequency spectra and inferred a common age of 6.8 (+-) 0.4 Gyr and common initial chemical composition
- Conclusion - Kepler asteroseismology gives precise stellar properties, larger datasets could allow for analysis of internal rotation, convective zone depth and stellar activity cycles
